From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Barnes <hello@jonbarnes.dev>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 20:20:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Three ways to get the reflections back

Patch 0018 of the renderer series (OoT: Recompiled).

The foliage rule took the reflections with it. They are cast off whatever the water probe finds,
and that probe is the only ray tracing the translucent instance mask; water here is a large
surface in the translucent z mode that reads depth and never writes it, with plenty of triangles,
which is the foliage signature exactly. Once the rule became the default, water stopped carrying
the bit the probe looks for.

The obvious answer, giving a reclassified draw both bits, was tried in the default and cost the
trees: "you just regressed all the fixes for trees back". Reading the shipped code did not explain
how, so it was reverted rather than defended.

So the candidates go on rungs, at the user's word: "keep my build as the working one that all tree
fixes were done and live and then add more options to test the fixes for reflections so i can
cycle them to see which retain tree work and allow refleections". The default is untouched and
stays where he confirmed it.

  27  the instance keeps both bits, which is the reverted attempt, offered to be observed
  28  the PROBE looks wider instead: no instance mask changes, so the trees cannot regress by
      construction, and water is found again through the depth bit it now carries
  29  both

28 is the one worth trying first, and it came out of writing down why 27 was risky rather than out
of patching forward. Its own risk is the opposite one: everything opaque carries the depth mask,
so the probe can now meet a horizontal floor, and the test that rejects anything not horizontal
and seen from above is all that stands between it and a reflective field.

All three classify exactly as the default does, so anything that changes in the trees is the
candidate's doing and nothing else's.

With the ray tracing row Off none of this runs.
---
diff --git a/src/shaders/Raytracing.hlsl b/src/shaders/Raytracing.hlsl
--- a/src/shaders/Raytracing.hlsl
+++ b/src/shaders/Raytracing.hlsl
@@ -281,7 +281,20 @@ void PrimaryRayGen() {
         water.shadedColor = 0.0f;
         water.hitVelocity = 0.0f;
         water.coverage = 1.0f;
-        TraceRay(SceneBVH, RAY_FLAG_NONE, TranslucentRayQueryMask, 0, 0, 0, probe, water);
+        // REFLECTION CANDIDATE B (patch 0018, rungs 28 and 29): the PROBE looks wider, rather than
+        // any instance's mask changing. Water carries the depth mask now that the foliage rule
+        // classifies it as solid, so a probe that also traces depth finds it again, and no tree's
+        // mask is touched, which means the tree work cannot regress by construction. That is the
+        // whole argument for it over candidate A, which was tried in the default and cost the
+        // trees for reasons the code did not explain.
+        //
+        // The risk is the opposite one and is why this is a rung too: everything opaque carries
+        // the depth mask, so the probe can now meet a horizontal FLOOR and the test below would
+        // call it water. The test is the only thing standing between this and a reflective field.
+        const uint probeMask = ((RtParams.tuneExperiment.x == 28.0f) || (RtParams.tuneExperiment.x == 29.0f))
+                                   ? (TranslucentRayQueryMask | DepthRayQueryMask)
+                                   : TranslucentRayQueryMask;
+        TraceRay(SceneBVH, RAY_FLAG_NONE, probeMask, 0, 0, 0, probe, water);
         // Water is a horizontal translucent surface seen from ABOVE (the hit normal faces the
         // ray, so it points up): a translucent surface seen from below (Death Mountain's ring
         // of smoke over Kakariko, a ceiling's haze) is not water, and the reflection pass drew
diff --git a/src/render/rt64_framebuffer_renderer.cpp b/src/render/rt64_framebuffer_renderer.cpp
--- a/src/render/rt64_framebuffer_renderer.cpp
+++ b/src/render/rt64_framebuffer_renderer.cpp
@@ -1918,6 +1918,14 @@ namespace RT64 {
                             // matched one of this game's trees. Kept as a rung rather than as the
                             // fallback, so the thing the ladder disproved can still be seen.
                             case 26: solidDraw = otherMode.cvgXAlpha() || (acMode == G_AC_THRESHOLD) || (acMode == G_AC_DITHER); break;
+                            // 27 to 29 are the DEFAULT's classification exactly, so the only
+                            // thing that differs between them and Off is the reflection
+                            // candidate each one turns on (above, and in the water probe).
+                            // That is what makes them a fair test: anything he sees change in
+                            // the trees is the candidate's doing and nothing else's.
+                            case 27:
+                            case 28:
+                            case 29: solidDraw = otherMode.zCmp() && manyTriangles; break;
                             // THE DEFAULT IS NOW THE RULE THAT WON (patch 0017), which is rung 25:
                             // a draw in the translucent z mode that READS depth and is more than a
                             // sprite is solid, and it casts. The user, 2026-09-30, on that rung:
@@ -1944,6 +1952,23 @@ namespace RT64 {
                         else {
                             raytracing.queryMask = (otherMode.zCmp() || otherMode.zUpd()) ? DepthRayQueryMask : NoDepthRayQueryMask;
                         }
+                        // REFLECTION CANDIDATE A (patch 0018, rungs 27 and 29). The reflections
+                        // are cast off whatever the water probe finds, and that probe is the only
+                        // ray tracing the translucent mask. Water here is a large surface in the
+                        // translucent z mode that reads depth and never writes it, with plenty of
+                        // triangles, which is the foliage signature exactly: once the foliage rule
+                        // became the default, water stopped carrying the bit the probe looks for
+                        // and every reflection went with it.
+                        //
+                        // Giving a reclassified draw BOTH bits is the obvious answer and it is on
+                        // a rung rather than in the default, because it was tried in the default
+                        // and the trees came back wrong (the user, 2026-09-30: "you just regressed
+                        // all the fixes for trees back"). Reading the code did not explain how, so
+                        // it is offered as something to observe rather than as a fix.
+                        if ((wasTranslucent && solidDraw) &&
+                            ((cutoutRule == 27) || (cutoutRule == 29))) {
+                            raytracing.queryMask |= TranslucentRayQueryMask;
+                        }
                         if (drawData.extraParams[call.callDesc.callIndex].shadowCatcherFactor > 0.0f) {
                             raytracing.queryMask |= ShadowCatcherRayQueryMask;
                         }
